How the engine works, in brief:
- Facts are extracted PER PAGE by a model that reads ONE page with no other context, then collapsed into a single `doc` object by each field's `agg`. The CEL reads `doc.<field>`.
- Contract entry shape: `name` (one of the required names), `type` ("boolean" | "number" | "string" | "string_list"), `agg`, `instruction` (ONE self-contained sentence judging a SINGLE page, answerable from that page alone, never reference other pages).
- `agg` semantics: "any"/"all" (boolean over pages; "all" counts only pages that mention it), "sum"/"max"/"min" (number), "union" (string_list), "first" (string), "pages" (boolean → ordered LIST of page numbers where true; the CEL reads it with all/exists macros and page arithmetic), "tagged_pages" (string_list of short canonical tags per page → LIST of {item, page} pairs; the macro reads `.item` and `.page`).
- Infer each field's type and agg FROM HOW THE CEL USES IT: `doc.x` alone or negated → boolean (any); compared to a number → number (sum for counts, max for a prescribed quantity); `size(doc.x)` → string_list (union); `doc.x.all(p, (p …) in doc.y)` with page arithmetic → boolean with agg "pages"; `.item`/`.page` members → string_list with agg "tagged_pages" (then the instruction must pin a canonical tag vocabulary: conventional name, lowercase, 1–3 words, standard form, and say what does not count as bearing the item).
- A number that a document PRESCRIBES (a required word count, a time limit) is read from what the page stipulates, never measured from the page itself.
- `doc.page_count` is supplied by the engine (the document's total number of pages) and is never a contract field, it will never appear in the names you must declare. Where the CEL compares a page number to it (`p == doc.page_count`, `p * 3 > doc.page_count * 2`), the rule is about ABSOLUTE position, so the field it iterates is a boolean with agg "pages" whose instruction describes only the page's CONTENT, never its position.
- If the CEL is conditional (`!doc.condition || …`), mark the condition field with `"gate": true`: when every gate field is falsy across a document the engine reports "not applicable" instead of a vacuous pass. Never mark the field that carries the verdict itself.

